course: dictionary
lesson: 15653
title: Formal words: therefrom
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: «Therefrom» is an old, formal word. It means from that, or from the thing or place that was just mentioned. You will meet it in legal papers, old books and very formal writing, so you need to understand it, but you will rarely need to use it yourself.
explain: Look for the word that comes before it. «The storm broke three windows. The repair bill therefrom was £200.» The storm is the thing just mentioned, so the bill from the storm is the one meant. Try the swap: «The repair bill from the storm was £200.» It still makes sense, so you have the meaning.
explain: Look for phrases with it. «Arising therefrom» means coming out of that, and «derived therefrom» means taken or got from that. Once you know the pattern, these phrases are easier to read, even though the words look hard.
fact: «Therefrom» means from that, or from that thing or place just mentioned.
fact: «The money and any profit therefrom» means the profit that comes from that money.
fact: «Arising therefrom» means coming out of it, and «derived therefrom» means taken or got from it.
fact: You will mostly meet «therefrom» in legal papers, old books and very formal writing.
fact: A good check is to swap in «from that» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The storm broke three windows. The repair bill therefrom was £200.» What does therefrom mean here? | Loudly and quickly | Under the roof | Before the storm | *From the storm
exercise: «The club may keep any profit therefrom.» What does therefrom point back to? | A new football | *The thing just mentioned, which is the money or event the profit came from | The club's name | The weather on Saturday
exercise: Which phrase means coming out of something? | *Arising therefrom | Lost in the woods | Very happy | Derived therefrom
test: «The river flooded the fields, and the bridge was damaged. Any repairs therefrom will take a year.» What does therefrom mean here? | Before the flood | Very quickly | *From the flood | Under the bridge
test: Where would you most often meet the word therefrom? | On a café menu | *In old legal papers and very formal writing | In a text message to a friend | In a playground game
exam: «Any profit therefrom belongs to the club.» What does therefrom mean? | Far away and cold | Loudly and quickly | Before that time | *From that: from the thing just mentioned
